pra: the zone_require panic was the Setup probe reading a port as a space

The 0159 boot finally captured the backtrace (ring16, and the panic
flush stole the eMMC lock after its 5 s wait and wrote it): 9 frames,
slide ~0, all resolving in the symtab:

  fleh_synchronous -> sleh_synchronous -> mach_syscall -> mach_msg2_trap
  -> mach_msg_trap_send -> ipc_kmsg_send -> pra_msg_probe (0153, inlined)
  -> get_bsdtask_info -> proc_require -> zone_id_require -> panic

addr 0x1a902fffff880 was the receiver task's bsd_info. The probe looked
up the destination port's receiver with
ip_get_receiver_ptr_noauth(port) and only checked the pointer against
IPC_SPACE_NULL - but ip_receiver is a union with ip_destination and is
an ipc_space_t only while ip_in_a_space(port). For a port mid-churn
(exec storm, union holding a destination PORT) the probe read a port as
a space, took garbage at the is_task offset as a task, and
get_bsdtask_info's proc_require panicked in zone_id_require. Every
zone_require panic since 0156 was this probe, not Apple code.

The probe also ran before ip_mq_lock(port), so even a correct in-space
check could race the union transition.

- pra_msg_probe is now called under ip_mq_lock(port), and extracts the
  receiver only when ip_in_a_space(port).
- pra_task_comm reads task_get_proc_raw instead of get_bsdtask_info:
  the raw accessor cannot panic, and NULL still means "kernel". A task
  pinned by a locked space has bsd_info NULL or live, so the raw read
  is safe for printing the name.
diff --git a/osfmk/ipc/ipc_kmsg.c b/osfmk/ipc/ipc_kmsg.c
--- a/osfmk/ipc/ipc_kmsg.c
+++ b/osfmk/ipc/ipc_kmsg.c
@@ -2895,7 +2895,18 @@ pra_task_comm(task_t task)
 	if (task == TASK_NULL) {
 		return "kernel";
 	}
-	info = get_bsdtask_info(task);
+	/*
+	 * task_get_proc_raw, not get_bsdtask_info: the latter proc_requires the
+	 * pointer, and proc_require panics through zone_id_require on anything
+	 * that is not a live zone element. The 0159 boot proved the receiver of
+	 * a mid-send port can yield exactly that (panic caller chain: ipc_kmsg
+	 * send -> pra_msg_probe -> get_bsdtask_info -> proc_require -> panic,
+	 * addr 0x1a902fffff880). The probe only wants the name: read the raw
+	 * bsd_info and give up on NULL. A caller that guarantees the task is
+	 * alive (the port lock below) guarantees the raw pointer is either NULL
+	 * or a live proc, so no further validation is needed here.
+	 */
+	info = task_get_proc_raw(task);
 	if (info == NULL) {
 		return "kernel";
 	}
@@ -2912,9 +2923,19 @@ pra_msg_probe(ipc_kmsg_t kmsg, ipc_port_t port)
 	const char *sname, *rname;
 	uint32_t i, free_slot = PRA_MSG_KEYS;
 
-	space = (ipc_space_t)ip_get_receiver_ptr_noauth(port);
-	if (space != IPC_SPACE_NULL) {
-		receiver = space->is_task;
+	/*
+	 * ip_receiver is a union with ip_destination: it is an ipc_space_t only
+	 * while ip_in_a_space(port). 0159 checked the pointer against NULL
+	 * instead and read a destination PORT as a space mid-churn - the
+	 * is_task offset then holds garbage, and get_bsdtask_info panicked in
+	 * proc_require. The caller holds ip_mq_lock(port) (see ipc_kmsg_send),
+	 * so both the in-space bit and the receiver pointer are stable here.
+	 */
+	if (ip_in_a_space(port)) {
+		space = (ipc_space_t)ip_get_receiver_ptr_noauth(port);
+		if (space != IPC_SPACE_NULL) {
+			receiver = space->is_task;
+		}
 	}
 	sname = pra_task_comm(sender);
 	rname = pra_task_comm(receiver);
@@ -3018,10 +3039,15 @@ ipc_kmsg_send(
 
 	port = hdr->msgh_remote_port;
 	assert(IP_VALID(port));
+	ip_mq_lock(port);
 #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_PRA)
+	/*
+	 * Under the port lock: the probe reads ip_in_a_space/ip_receiver,
+	 * which transition exactly under ip_mq_lock. 0159 called it before
+	 * the lock and raced the space<->destination union mid-churn.
+	 */
 	pra_msg_probe(kmsg, port);
 #endif
-	ip_mq_lock(port);
 
 	/*
 	 * If the destination has been guarded with a reply context, and the
